Contribution Forks
------------------

A Contribution Fork is permitted, and it may be public. On most code-hosting
platforms — GitHub included — a pull request cannot be opened from a private
fork to a public repository, so a public fork is the only workable route for
the contribution path this License invites.

A Contribution Fork must:

  (a) exist to prepare Contributions to the Canonical Project, and not be
      presented, promoted, or maintained as an independent project, an
      alternative distribution, or a continuation of the Software;

  (b) keep the platform's fork relationship to the Canonical Project intact,
      and carry this License unmodified;

  (c) not be used to publish binary releases, package-registry entries, or
      hosted services; and

  (d) be deleted or made private once its Contributions have been merged or
      abandoned, and in any case within 90 days of the last of them being
      closed.

A fork that stops meeting these conditions is not a Contribution Fork, and
Section 3(b) applies to it in full.

